phoenix: qcom_smbx fix recharge after termination + overvoltage status read

Two fixes for the SMB5 charger path used by PM6150 on phoenix:

- smbx_ov_status() tested the register *address* (reg) instead of the
  value read from it (val), so battery-overvoltage detection could
  never trigger. Test val instead.

- After a charge termination (TERMINATE_CHARGE/INHIBIT_CHARGE) the
  hardware only restarts charging once the recharge threshold is
  crossed again. On phoenix the QG fuel gauge SOC is unreliable (no
  calibrated battery profile), so a freshly plugged charger can be
  left with an input current limit of 0 and no charging while the
  phone drains and reports "Full". When APSD completes while the
  charger is in such a state, bounce the charging enable bit (and
  make sure USBIN is not suspended) to force a fresh charge cycle.

Signed-off-by: phoenix port <fix@me>

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_smbx.c b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_smbx.c
index 1d1d041..8a3d751 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_smbx.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_smbx.c
@@ -372,7 +372,7 @@ static int smbx_ov_status(struct smb_chip *chip)
 	if (rc)
 		return rc;
 
-	return !!(reg & mask);
+	return !!(val & mask);
 }
 
 static int smb_get_prop_status(struct smb_chip *chip, int *val)
@@ -508,6 +508,7 @@ static int smb_set_current_limit(struct smb_chip *chip, unsigned int val)
 static void smb_status_change_work(struct work_struct *work)
 {
 	unsigned int charger_type, current_ua;
+	unsigned int stat;
 	int usb_online = 0;
 	int count, rc;
 	struct smb_chip *chip;
@@ -554,6 +555,35 @@ static void smb_status_change_work(struct work_struct *work)
 	}
 
 	smb_set_current_limit(chip, current_ua);
+
+	/*
+	 * If the charger is in terminate/inhibit state from an earlier
+	 * charge cycle, the hardware only restarts charging once the
+	 * recharge threshold is crossed again. Devices whose fuel gauge
+	 * data is uncalibrated may never report a stable SOC, leaving the
+	 * input current limit at 0 and the battery draining while the
+	 * charger reports "Full". Kick a fresh charge cycle whenever we
+	 * (re)detect a charger in such a state.
+	 */
+	rc = regmap_read(chip->regmap,
+			 chip->base + BATTERY_CHARGER_STATUS_1, &stat);
+	if (rc >= 0) {
+		stat &= BATTERY_CHARGER_STATUS_MASK;
+		if (stat == TERMINATE_CHARGE || stat == INHIBIT_CHARGE) {
+			dev_dbg(chip->dev,
+				"charge terminated, forcing recharge\n");
+			regmap_update_bits(chip->regmap,
+					   chip->base + USBIN_CMD_IL,
+					   USBIN_SUSPEND_BIT, 0);
+			regmap_write(chip->regmap,
+				     chip->base + CHARGING_ENABLE_CMD, 0);
+			usleep_range(1000, 2000);
+			regmap_write(chip->regmap,
+				     chip->base + CHARGING_ENABLE_CMD,
+				     CHARGING_ENABLE_CMD_BIT);
+		}
+	}
+
 	power_supply_changed(chip->chg_psy);
 }
 
